1 : git --version (gives git version, we use it to make sure get is in our pc)
2 : git config --global user.name "name" (we use it to set username for get it can be different from github username)
3 : git config --global user.email "email" (we use it to set email for git it must be same as github's)
4 : create repository in github
5 : git init (creates a dossier called git that follows the changes on the project)
6 : git status (give the status of the folder as ex: if git dossier is created or not, if files ar tracked or not)
7 : git add name of the file "or" . for a lot of files (it adds the files to the staging area that makes files ready to git commited)
8 : git commit -m "commit name" (acts like a save game for the changes that happened after writing it, i can also see the date of the versions)
9: git remote add orgin https://repository_link (add's the project created in the pc in the repository created in github)
10 : git push -u origin branch_name (adds the commits in the local to github)